{"name":"Blue Damselfly","hex":"#2fa1da","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"damselfly","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various insects of the suborder Zygoptera that have long slender bodies, and are similar to dragonflies but having wings folded when at rest."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/damselfly"}],"entries":[{"title":"Damselfly","blocks":[{"text":"Damselflies are flying insects of the suborder Zygoptera in the order Odonata. They are similar to dragonflies, which form the suborder Anisoptera, but are usually smaller and have slimmer bodies. Most species fold the wings along the body when at rest, unlike dragonflies which hold the wings flat and away from the body. Damselflies have existed since the Late Jurassic, and are found on every continent except Antarctica."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Beautiful_demoiselle_%28Calopteryx_virgo%29_male_3.jpg/330px-Beautiful_demoiselle_%28Calopteryx_virgo%29_male_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damselfly","wikidata":"Q230502"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Ischnura_heterosticta02.jpg/500px-Ischnura_heterosticta02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Ischnura_heterosticta02.jpg/1280px-Ischnura_heterosticta02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Ischnura_heterosticta02.jpg/500px-Ischnura_heterosticta02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIschnura_heterosticta02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0e/Pseudagrion_caffrum_abdomen_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0e/Pseudagrion_caffrum_abdomen_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Terminal segments of the abdomen of a male damselfly (Pseudagrion caffrum) showing segments 8–10 (S8, S9, S10), the upper or superior appendages or cerci (c) and the inferior appendages or paraprocts (p)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APseudagrion_caffrum_abdomen_detail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Calopteryx_splendens_qtl7.jpg/500px-Calopt